          "id": "regulatory_barriers",
          "status": "absent",
          "evidence": "ServiceTitan operates in field service management SaaS with no industry-specific regulatory requirements. The company has standard enterprise SaaS compliance (SOC 2, etc.) but these are table stakes, not competitive barriers. No patent portfolio with active enforcement has been disclosed in public filings that would create a regulatory/IP moat at the category level.",
          "evidence_source": "agent_estimate",
          "trajectory": "stable"
        },
        {
          "id": "cost_structure",
          "status": "absent",
          "evidence": "ServiceTitan operates at premium pricing ($245–$500/technician/month plus $5K–$50K+ implementation fees) and has approximately 3,414 employees as of January 2026 — a heavily staffed model with high cost to serve. Its public company structure and scale create some infrastructure leverage, but the overall cost structure is high relative to lean SMB competitors like Jobber or Housecall Pro. ServiceTitan competes on feature depth and brand, not cost efficiency.",
          "evidence_source": "agent_estimate",
          "trajectory": "stable"
        },
        {
          "id": "brand_reputation",
          "status": "strong",
          "evidence": "ServiceTitan is the category-defining brand in trades field service management. It was the first FSM company to IPO (NASDAQ: TTAN, December 2024 at ~$7.59B), which substantially elevated brand credibility. It is the first competitor mentioned by investors evaluating any trades FSM deal, per the landscape analysis. Customers seek it out by name, it runs a major annual customer conference (Pantheon), and it is the reference point that all SMB FSM competitors position against ('simpler than ServiceTitan' is how Jobber, HCP, and Tessivane all frame themselves).",
          "evidence_source": "agent_estimate",
          "trajectory": "stable"
        }
